From: EglantinePS5 <Vampinity@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: up: prep unread handle, a kept translation that never read a descriptor's texture handle is not refused for it (EDEN_UP_PREP_UNREAD_HANDLE)

DRAFT, not built. Our own work (shader prep refusals on the door route; the skip matches what
zortos293/Lichii #896 does by never looking at such a descriptor). EDEN_UP_PREP_UNREAD_HANDLE
(boot, read once; exact 1 or 2, anything else off); off by default. 1: in AnswersHoldFor a
descriptor neither of whose two handle words was read by the kept translation is skipped and
counted, everything else is checked as before, so the kept SPIR-V is used (about 1.5 ms instead
of a 26-96 ms translation on the GPU thread). 2: count only, still refused as "unread handle
(count only)". Both split the handle refusals ("texture handle shift", "texture handle half
read"), name every refused or rescued pipeline key (at most 512 lines a session) and add a 10 s
line "Unread texture handles in the last 10 s". EDEN_UP_PREP_UNREAD_HANDLE_VERIFY=1 (boot, needs
=1): every rescued pipeline is also translated fresh, drawn with the fresh one and compared
(SPIR-V, infos, and whether the fresh translation read a skipped word); =2 also flips one byte of
the first rescued pipeline's kept SPIR-V (expect 1 differ). Line "Unread handles verified so far",
also with EDEN_UP_SHADER_PREP_VERIFY=1.
Not in TranslationSwitches (the shader-store stamp): it changes no translation, so every arm
shares one store; the names are only in vk_pipeline_prep.cpp, which the switch-list check skips.
Off: AnswersHoldFor gets a null pointer (same reasons, same log); on the kept-record miss path
only (a few hundred a run, never per draw): a cached switch read and a few locals.

Reviewed draft (two reviews, "ready with small corrections"), corrections applied here: the check
counts a pipeline only after its comparison finished (compared_same starts false); the one fault
flip is spent only after a finished comparison; the verified line also prints with
EDEN_UP_SHADER_PREP_VERIFY=1; the half-read comment is corrected (the integer check of the
previous descriptor at the same first word can leave the second word unread, so a few "half read"
refusals are possible and stay refused); the error lines about skipped words read are capped by
lines, not pipelines; the per-stage bookkeeping runs only with the switch on.
